Kept this version close to the way the result looks on paper. After sorting, a value should equal its index. The first place where that is not true gives the missing number. If every position matches, the missing value is `n`.

For example, sorting `[3, 0, 1]` gives `[0, 1, 3]`, so index `2` is the answer. Sorting changes the input array, which is the main trade-off here.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int missingNumber(vector<int>& nums) {
        sort(nums.begin(), nums.end());

        for (int i = 0; i < static_cast<int>(nums.size()); i++) {
            if (nums[i] != i) {
                return i;
            }
        }

        return nums.size();
    }
};
```

Sorting takes `O(n log n)` time. Apart from the sorting implementation, the code uses `O(1)` extra space.

Instead of sorting the whole array, this version places each value at the index with the same number. Value `n` has no matching index because the last index is `n - 1`, so it can be skipped.

Dry run for `[3, 0, 1]`: `0` moves to index `0`, `1` moves to index `1`, and the empty index `2` is found during the final scan. If all indexes are filled correctly, `n` is missing.

The swaps change the input array, so this is only suitable when modifying `nums` is allowed.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int missingNumber(vector<int>& nums) {
        int n = nums.size();
        int index = 0;

        while (index < n) {
            int value = nums[index];

            if (value < n && nums[value] != value) {
                swap(nums[index], nums[value]);
            } else {
                index++;
            }
        }

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            if (nums[i] != i) {
                return i;
            }
        }

        return n;
    }
};
```

Each value is moved a limited number of times, giving `O(n)` time overall. The extra space is `O(1)`.

XOR is a nice fit here because every number that appears twice cancels out. The complete range is `0` through `n`, while the array contains all but one of those values. XORing both groups leaves only the missing value.

For `nums = [3, 0, 1]`, the repeated values cancel and `2` remains. This also handles an empty array and cases where `0` or `n` is missing.

```java
class Solution {
    public int missingNumber(int[] nums) {
        int result = nums.length;

        for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {
            result ^= i;
            result ^= nums[i];
        }

        return result;
    }
}
```

The time complexity is `O(n)` and the extra space is `O(1)`. The input array is not changed.

This uses a set to remember the values that are present. Then it checks every number from `0` to `n`. The first value not found in the set is missing.

The empty input is also fine: the set stays empty, and the loop immediately returns `0`. The extra set is easy to understand, but it needs more memory than an XOR or sum calculation.

```java
class Solution {
    public int missingNumber(int[] nums) {
        java.util.HashSet<Integer> seen = new java.util.HashSet<>();

        for (int value : nums) {
            seen.add(value);
        }

        for (int value = 0; value <= nums.length; value++) {
            if (!seen.contains(value)) {
                return value;
            }
        }

        return -1;
    }
}
```

Expected time is `O(n)` and the extra space is `O(n)`. The array is not modified.

A counting array makes the check very direct. There are `n + 1` possible values, from `0` to `n`, so each number can mark its own position. The position that stays unmarked is the answer.

Dry run: with `[3, 0, 1]`, positions `3`, `0`, and `1` are marked. Position `2` is still empty, so the function returns `2`.

```python
class Solution:
    def missingNumber(self, nums):
        count = [False] * (len(nums) + 1)

        for value in nums:
            count[value] = True

        for value in range(len(count)):
            if not count[value]:
                return value

        return -1
```

The two loops together take `O(n)` time, and the table uses `O(n)` extra space. The array itself is left unchanged.

